# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the tsan-6.9 test preset.
#
# Qt comes as a binary build without ThreadSanitizer. Its mutexes, wait
# conditions and atomics inside the libraries are invisible to the sanitizer,
# so memory that Qt hands from one thread to another looks unsynchronised.
#
# The preset therefore also sets ignore_noninstrumented_modules=1. The
# sanitizer then ignores the calls to malloc, free, memcpy and the like that
# come from a library built without it, such as Qt, GLib or libdbus. Without
# the option it pairs, for example, a QMetaCallEvent that Qt D-Bus allocates
# on the main thread with its deletion by Qt Core on the D-Bus thread after
# Qt's event queue passed it on. Accesses in project code stay checked.
#
# An entry below covers only a report whose accesses all lie in Qt code. A
# report about memory that project code reads or writes is never suppressed
# here.

# QFuture::unwrap(). Qt's continuation code in qfuture_impl.h builds the
# continuation for the inner future on one thread and runs it on the thread
# that finishes that future. QFutureInterfaceBase::setContinuation() and
# runContinuation() order the two under continuationMutex inside Qt Core,
# which the sanitizer cannot see. Every reported access lies in that Qt code;
# the project lambdas around it run in frames of their own.
race:QtPrivate::UnwrapHandler::unwrapImpl
